{
  "summary": "P1 SECURITY - VIP (Marcus Reyes, CFO, user_id 2). Reported: several unrequested Duo pushes early this morning; denied them but may have approved one by accident. Sign-in logs confirm MFA fatigue attack: VPN attempts from Singapore, SG, IP 192.0.2.77 (hosting network) at 2026-10-10 08:10, 08:11, 08:12 UTC = mfa_denied, then 08:14 UTC (04:14 ET) = SUCCESS via Duo push. No failed_password entries, so attacker had the correct password. All other activity is normal (New York, corporate/residential, business hours). Duo enrolled on user's phone (enrolled 2025-12-15). Account currently active, sign-in not yet blocked, VPN access true. Actions queued for tech approval: block_sign_in (approval 1), reset_password (approval 2; deliver temp password by phone after callback to number on file). Still needed: approve block + reset urgently; kill active VPN session(s) from 192.0.2.77 and revoke tokens/sessions; investigate what the attacker accessed over VPN after 08:14 UTC (finance systems, file shares, mailbox rules/forwarding); determine how the password was obtained (phishing? reuse?); consider Duo number-matching/re-enrollment check; check laptop BL-LT-0102; notify security team.",
  "reason": "P1 security incident with confirmed suspicious successful sign-in on an executive account; KB-029/KB-004 require escalation."
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Marcus, thanks for reporting this so quickly. You were right to be worried. Our records show that someone outside the company had your password. Early Saturday morning, around 4:10–4:14 am Eastern, they tried to get onto the VPN several times. You denied three pushes, but the last attempt went through, which fits with a push being approved by accident. Please treat your password as stolen.\n\nHere's what happens next: we've asked a technician to approve two urgent steps. The first blocks all sign-ins and VPN on your account so the outsider is cut off. The second sets a new temporary password. Neither step has been done yet. Once they are, a technician will call you on the number we have on file to confirm it's you and give you the temporary password over the phone. We will never send it by email. You'll be locked out until then. Our security team is also looking into what was accessed.\n\nIn the meantime:\n- Don't approve any Duo push you didn't start yourself, even if more keep coming. Deny them and let us know.\n- If you used this password anywhere else, change it there too.\n- Don't delete any suspicious emails you may have gotten recently. Security may want to look at them.\n\nWe're treating this as top priority and will be in touch soon.",
  "internal_note": "P1 security incident on a VIP account (CFO). This looks like an MFA fatigue attack, and the account was likely compromised. Sign-in logs: VPN attempts from Singapore, IP 192.0.2.77 (hosting network). 3x mfa_denied at 08:10, 08:11 and 08:12 UTC on 2026-10-10, then a SUCCESS at 08:14 UTC (04:14 ET) via Duo push. There were no failed_password entries, so the attacker had the correct password. All other sign-ins look normal (New York, corporate/residential networks, around 09:14 ET). Duo is enrolled on the user's phone. The account is still active and not blocked, and VPN access is on. Queued for approval: block_sign_in (approval #1) and reset_password (approval #2). Give the temp password by phone only, after callback to the number on file. Escalated to Tier 2 (escalation #1) to: kill active sessions, investigate what was accessed after 08:14 UTC (finance systems, mailbox rules/forwarding), find how the password leaked, and check laptop BL-LT-0102. KB-031 is referenced by KB-004 but didn't come up in search, so I followed KB-029.",
